---
/** The privacy notice, the same for every site: they are one service. */
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SITES, type Topic } from '@isithot/core';
import { siteNames } from '../lib/topics';

interface Props {
  topic: Topic;
}
const { topic } = Astro.props;
const site = SITES[topic].name;
---

<Content
  title="Privacy"
  path="/privacy"
  description={`What ${site} collects: an approximate, rounded location to look up the forecast. No accounts. Also what our iPhone app keeps for notifications.`}
  topic={topic}
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p>
    <em>Draft for review. Last updated 23 October 2026.</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    {siteNames(topic)} are one service, built to need as little about you as possible. This notice
    covers all four, and our iPhone app (below). There are no accounts and we do not sell personal
    data.
  </p>

  <h2>Location</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Approximate location.</strong> When you open the site, our hosting provider (Netlify)
      estimates a rough location from your IP address so we can show a forecast straight away. We
      use the town and coordinates to look up the weather. We do not store your IP address with
      them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Device location.</strong> Only if you allow it in your browser. Your coordinates are
      rounded to about 5 km (0.05°) in your browser before they are sent to us, and we never log
      them alongside your IP address.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Searches.</strong> Place names you search for are sent to our forecast provider,
      Open-Meteo, to find matching places. Popular searches are cached for a week.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Your browser's local storage keeps your last chosen place, your units (°C or °F, which also sets
    millimetres or inches of rain, and centimetres or inches of snow, and separately mph or km/h for
    wind) and the last answer so the page appears instantly next time. This never leaves your
    device, and you can clear it at any time in your browser settings. Each site keeps its own copy.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else processes data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Netlify hosts the site and processes requests, including IP addresses, to serve pages.</li>
    <li>
      Open-Meteo receives the rounded coordinates of the forecast grid cell (not your IP address)
      when our cache does not already have a forecast for it.
    </li>
    <li>NASA POWER receives the coordinates of a 50 km climate grid cell.</li>
    <li>
      Cloudflare Web Analytics counts page views without cookies or personal identifiers, if
      enabled.
    </li>
    <li>
      Google AdSense shows one advert, only after you have made a choice in the consent banner where
      the law requires it. See our <a href="/cookies">cookie notice</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Our apps</h2>
  <p>
    The willitsnow.today app for iPhone works like this website: it sends the place you ask about (a
    town, or your location rounded to about 5&nbsp;km) and your units to our server to get the
    forecast. As on the website, until you choose a place it uses a rough location from your IP
    address, place searches go to Open-Meteo, and it keeps your place, units, recent places and last
    answer on your iPhone. It has no accounts.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Notifications.</strong> If you turn on notifications in the app, we keep your device's
      notification token for the app, the place you chose (a town, or a location rounded to about
      5&nbsp;km), its time zone, your units and which notifications you asked for, so we can tell
      you when snow is likely. With them we keep an ID made from the token, the app's version,
      whether it is a developer build, when you turned notifications on and when the app last
      checked in with us. It checks in when you open it: at most once a week, unless you change
      something. We delete all of this when you turn notifications off in the app, when Apple tells
      us the app is no longer installed, or 90 days after the app last checked in, so about 90 days
      after you last opened it. If you turn notifications off in your iPhone's Settings instead, we
      delete it the next time you open the app. For a few days after each notification and each
      test, we also keep a note that it was sent, even once the rest is deleted, so a notification
      is never sent twice and tests keep to 3 an hour; it holds no token or place. Notifications are
      sent through Apple's push service. Our server and its storage are run by Netlify, in the
      United States. In the app, Settings → Your privacy shows the ID and the main things we keep,
      and "Turn off notifications and delete my data" deletes them.
    </li>
    <!-- prettier-ignore -->
    <li>
      <strong>Ads in the app.</strong> The free version shows ads from Google AdMob. In the UK and
      the EEA, Google's consent message asks for your choice first, and Settings → Privacy choices
      lets you change it. Google may collect device identifiers, advertising data, usage and
      diagnostics, and an approximate location from your IP address. See
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>. The
      one-off purchase turns the ads off.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Purchases.</strong> Apple handles purchases. We never see your payment details.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under UK GDPR you can ask what we hold about you and ask us to delete it. The websites keep no
    personal data tied to you, so for them there is usually nothing to find. If you use
    notifications in the app, Settings → Your privacy shows the main things we keep and "Turn off
    notifications and delete my data" deletes them, or you can email us the ID it shows. Contact:{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</Content>
